;;; A wildcard `_` in an hrule's `:match` proposes nothing — M1e2 **W20**,
;;; register row `W20/wild-in-hrule` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), found by
;;; S1e2.2.20 (2026-11-05) probing the case's mechanism.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a b)`, and one hrule that proposes `(tag ?x)` for each
;;; `holds` fact, whatever its second argument: the pattern writes `_` there.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An hrule's `:match` is a rule's pattern, so `_` matches any value: the
;;; hrule proposes `(tag a)`, H is that one candidate, nothing refutes, and R
;;; is `∅` and `{(tag a)}`. It is the answer of `wild-in-hrule-twin.ein`,
;;; this file with `_` written as `?any`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.20 H was empty: `_` was an opaque slot that matched nothing,
;;; so the hrule matched no fact and proposed no candidate. `ein solve FILE`
;;; at `c73a0796` printed *no candidate: R is {∅} or empty, decided by root
;;; alone*, and R was `{∅}`, in a release build and a debug one. Since then
;;; `_` matches any value, the hrule proposes `(tag a)`, and the program
;;; answers its twin's R.

(relation holds Node Node) (relation tag Node)
(holds a b)
(hrule h () :match (holds ?x _) :assert (tag ?x))
